---
/**
 * AI prompt footprint calculator
 *
 * Readers build their usage from rows of (model x output length x count per
 * day), which sum into one daily and annual carbon and water cost. The page
 * then sets that against their own footprint (where they live, diet, flying,
 * with AI carbon on their regional grid), next to everyday things, and over a
 * year against bigger lifestyle choices. State is saved to the URL for sharing.
 *
 * Per-model energy / carbon / water estimates (including uncertainty ranges)
 * come from the EcoLogits library (v0.10), the engine behind the EcoLogits
 * calculator at huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator. All
 * other figures are documented in the methodology section at the bottom.
 */
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const pageTitle = 'What does your AI use cost?';
const pageDescription =
  'Build up how many prompts you send each day to GPT, Claude, and Gemini, at any output length, and see the combined carbon and water cost set against your own footprint, everyday things, and bigger lifestyle changes.';
const pageUrl = 'https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint';

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: pageTitle,
    description: pageDescription,
    url: pageUrl,
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com' },
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
    about: [
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Artificial intelligence' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Carbon footprint' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Water footprint' },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Visuals', item: 'https://andymasley.com/visuals' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: pageTitle, item: pageUrl },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  canonicalUrl={pageUrl}
  ogTitle={pageTitle}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <article class="aipf" id="aipf">
    <header class="aipf-header">
      <h1 class="aipf-title">What your AI use actually costs</h1>
      <p class="aipf-dek">
        Enter an average day of AI use. It adds up into one carbon and water cost, set against your
        own footprint and the everyday things around it. Figures from
        <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a>,
        an open-source project that estimates the energy, carbon, and water behind each AI model.
        All other numbers are sourced in the methodology at the bottom. The cost of training is
        excluded because it's uncertain, but it does not add meaningful emissions or water to your
        personal footprint if you divide the cost by the number of users. See
        <a href="https://blog.andymasley.com/p/training-ai-models-doesnt-emit-that" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here for my estimates of how much training adds to your footprint</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="aipf-toolbar-top">
      <div class="aipf-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Footprint type">
        <span class="aipf-toggle-label">Show</span>
        <div class="aipf-showing">
          <button type="button" class="aipf-metric is-active" data-metric="carbon">💨 Carbon</button>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-metric" data-metric="water">💧 Water</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="aipf-report-btn" id="aipf-report"
        title="Generate a report with citations of exactly how my AI use as entered here contributes to my carbon emissions.">
        Generate a personalized cited report of my AI emissions
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- Tool: inputs on the left, the answer on the right -->
    <div class="aipf-tool">
      <div class="aipf-in" aria-label="Your daily AI usage">
        <div class="aipf-in-head"><span>Your AI use</span><span class="aipf-in-sub">per day</span></div>
        <div class="aipf-rows-head" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>Model</span><span>Typical output</span><span>Prompts/day</span><span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="aipf-rows" id="aipf-rows"></div>
        <button type="button" class="aipf-addrow" id="aipf-addrow">+ add another</button>
        <p class="aipf-running" id="aipf-running" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <div class="aipf-writes">
          <p class="aipf-writes-h">What it writes</p>
          <p class="aipf-words" id="aipf-words" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
        <p class="aipf-tools">
          <button type="button" class="aipf-link" id="aipf-reset">Reset</button>
          <span class="aipf-tools-sep">·</span>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-link" id="aipf-share">Copy link</button>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="aipf-out" aria-label="How it compares">
        <div class="aipf-out-head">
          <p class="aipf-life">
            vs. a day for someone in
            <select id="aipf-loc" aria-label="Where you live"></select> who lives in
            <span class="aipf-nw"><select id="aipf-home" aria-label="Your home"></select>,</span> drives
            <span class="aipf-nw"><select id="aipf-drive" aria-label="How much you drive"></select>,</span> eats
            <span class="aipf-nw"><select id="aipf-diet" aria-label="Your diet"></select>,</span> and flies
            <select id="aipf-fly" aria-label="How much you fly"></select>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="aipf-verdict" id="aipf-headline" aria-live="polite"></div>
        <div class="aipf-mini">
          <p class="aipf-mini-h" id="aipf-daily-sub"></p>
          <div class="aipf-bars aipf-bars--compact" id="aipf-daily-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
        </div>
        <p class="aipf-fine" id="aipf-grid-note"></p>
        <p class="aipf-fine">Excludes training, image and video generation, and retries.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Below: a year, split into emissions added vs cuts you could make -->
    <div class="aipf-below" aria-label="In a year, ways you add">
      <h2 class="aipf-h2" id="aipf-add-title">In a year, ways you add emissions</h2>
      <p class="aipf-sub" id="aipf-add-sub"></p>
      <div class="aipf-bars" id="aipf-add-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="aipf-below" aria-label="In a year, ways you can cut">
      <h2 class="aipf-h2" id="aipf-cut-title">In a year, ways you can cut emissions</h2>
      <p class="aipf-sub" id="aipf-cut-sub"></p>
      <div class="aipf-bars" id="aipf-cut-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </div>

    <details class="aipf-method">
      <summary>How these numbers are made</summary>
      <div class="aipf-method-body">
        <p>
          <strong>Per-model AI estimates.</strong> Energy, carbon, and water per prompt are
          computed with the open-source <a href="https://ecologits.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a>
          library (v0.10), the engine behind the
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits calculator</a>;
          the model list is current as of June 2026. EcoLogits estimates electricity from each
          model's (active) parameter count and output length, and adds the embodied impact of
          building the hardware. The low to high range is EcoLogits' own 95% confidence interval,
          mostly uncertainty in the parameter counts of closed models. Water is its
          water-consumption footprint — water actually consumed (evaporated in data-center cooling
          and in generating the electricity), not withdrawn and returned. Every water figure on this
          page is consumptive, so they compare on the same basis.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Carbon on your grid.</strong> EcoLogits reports carbon on the global average grid,
          about 0.59 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e/kWh. This page rescales the electricity to where you live
          using grid carbon intensities from
          <a href="https://ember-energy.org/latest-insights/global-electricity-review-2024/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ember's 2024 Global Electricity Review</a>
          and <a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/carbon-intensity-electricity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Our World in Data</a>:
          roughly 380 (US), 215 (EU), 125 (UK), 580 (China), 700 (India) g CO<sub>2</sub>e/kWh.
          Water stays on the global basis.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Output, words, and code.</strong> Each row maps to an EcoLogits output-token count:
          tweet (50), short email (170), article summary (250), chatbot reply (400), 5-page report
          (5,000), long document (15,000), coding or agent session (100,000), full novel (500,000).
          Cost scales with length, so long generations dominate. Counts are per day; annual figures
          multiply by 365. Words assume
          <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/4936856-what-are-tokens-and-how-to-count-them" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI's ~0.75 words per token</a>,
          read at
          <a href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/8647789" target="_blank" rel="noopener">238 words a minute (Brysbaert 2019)</a>.
          A coding session is counted as code at about 10 tokens a line, not as prose.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Your footprint.</strong> "Where you live" is a regional baseline for goods,
          services, and shared infrastructure (per-capita consumption from
          <a href="https://ourworldindata.org/co2-emissions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Our World in Data</a>);
          home energy and driving are separate so they do not double-count.
          <strong>Home</strong> runs from a small apartment to a big house, about 1.5 to 7 t
          CO<sub>2</sub>e a year (<a href="https://www.eia.gov/consumption/residential/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EIA RECS</a>;
          <a href="https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1922205117" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Goldstein et al. 2020</a>).
          <strong>Driving</strong> uses
          <a href="https://www.epa.gov/greenvehicles/greenhouse-gas-emissions-typical-passenger-vehicle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EPA's ~400 g CO<sub>2</sub>/mile</a>
          across roughly 3,000 to 25,000 miles a year (<a href="https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/policyinformation/statistics/2023/vm1.cfm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FHWA</a>).
          <strong>Diet</strong> uses food footprints from
          <a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aaq0216" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Poore &amp; Nemecek (2018)</a>
          and <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s43016-023-00795-w" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scarborough et al. (2023)</a>:
          about 1.05 t CO<sub>2</sub>e a year for a vegan diet up to 3.2 t for a heavy-meat one.
          <strong>Flying</strong> is anchored to a transatlantic round trip of about 1.6 t
          (<a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa7541" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wynes &amp; Nicholas 2017</a>);
          the rarely / sometimes / often options are roughly 0.5, 1.5, and 5 such trips a year.
          The <strong>water footprint</strong> is anchored to national per-capita totals from the
          <a href="https://tools.waterfootprint.org/national-explorer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Water Footprint Network (Mekonnen &amp; Hoekstra 2011)</a>,
          about 2,840 m<sup>3</sup>/yr for the US down to ~1,070 for China, and split into food (the
          diet knob), goods and services (location), and direct household use (home) following the
          roughly
          <a href="https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1109936109" target="_blank" rel="noopener">92% food / 4% goods / 4% domestic</a>
          breakdown they report; a
          <a href="https://theconversation.com/meat-free-diets-could-cut-our-water-footprint-in-half-say-scientists-102926" target="_blank" rel="noopener">plant-based diet cuts the food share by about half</a>.
          These are consumption figures, dominated by the green and blue water actually used up to
          grow food and make goods (grey pollution-dilution water is a minor part), so they sit on
          the same water-consumed basis as the AI figure, and pure withdrawals that return to the
          system are left out. It is mostly virtual agricultural water rather than data-center
          cooling, so the comparison shows scale, not identical accounting.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Lifestyle cuts (the yearly chart).</strong> The "saved" figures come from the
          <a href="https://www.founderspledge.com/research/climate-and-lifestyle-report" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Founders Pledge Climate &amp; Lifestyle report</a>,
          drawing on
          <a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa7541" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wynes &amp; Nicholas (2017)</a>
          (living car-free 2.4 t, avoiding a transatlantic flight 1.6 t, hang-drying, recycling, LED
          bulbs) and
          <a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/ab8589" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ivanova et al. (2020)</a>
          (green electricity, heat-pump heating, a home retrofit, electric and hybrid cars).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Everyday things.</strong> A cup of coffee: ~0.21 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e and
          <a href="https://www.weforum.org/stories/2019/03/hidden-water-in-your-cup-of-coffee/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">140 L of water</a>.
          An hour on a <a href="https://www.playstation.com/en-us/legal/ecodesign/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PS5 (~200 W)</a>;
          3 minutes in a <a href="https://www.siliconvalleypower.com/residents/save-energy/appliance-energy-use-chart" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1,200 W microwave</a>;
          a mile in a gas car (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/greenvehicles/greenhouse-gas-emissions-typical-passenger-vehicle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EPA, ~0.40 kg</a>);
          a 10-minute hot shower (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/watersense/showerheads" target="_blank" rel="noopener">~21 gal, EPA WaterSense</a>, plus water heating);
          a <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/products/dishwashers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dishwasher load (≤3.5 gal, ENERGY STAR)</a>;
          a <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/products/clothes_dryers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dryer load (~3 kWh)</a>;
          <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1530-9290.2011.00414.x" target="_blank" rel="noopener">printing a 400-page book (~2.7 kg, Wells 2012)</a>
          with <a href="https://waterfootprint.org/resources/Report46-WaterFootprintPaper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">paper's water footprint</a>;
          a beef burger (~3 kg, <a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aaq0216" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Poore &amp; Nemecek</a>; ~1,700 L at
          <a href="https://watercalculator.org/footprint/the-hidden-water-in-everyday-products/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">15,400 L/kg beef</a>);
          a <a href="https://www.apple.com/environment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">new smartphone (~70 kg, Apple)</a>
          needing <a href="https://www.compareandrecycle.co.uk/blog/world-water-week-how-much-water-does-your-smartphone-use" target="_blank" rel="noopener">~12,800 L</a>;
          a <a href="https://www.levistrauss.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/Full-LCA-Results-Deck-FINAL.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pair of jeans (~33 kg, Levi's)</a>
          at ~10,000 L (<a href="https://watercalculator.org/footprint/the-hidden-water-in-everyday-products/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Water Footprint Network</a>).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>How the appliance figures are derived.</strong> The coffee, PS5, microwave, shower,
          dishwasher, and dryer carbon numbers are each that item's measured electricity (cited
          above) times the US grid (~0.38 kg CO<sub>2</sub>/kWh), so they fall on cleaner grids.
          Two depend heavily on assumptions: a cup of coffee is
          <a href="https://theconversation.com/coffee-heres-the-carbon-cost-of-your-daily-cup-and-how-to-make-it-climate-friendly-152629" target="_blank" rel="noopener">~0.05 kg black up to ~0.5 kg for a large latte</a>
          (we use ~0.2 kg), and a 10-minute hot shower is roughly 0.2 kg on a gas water heater up to
          1.5 kg on electric resistance (we use ~0.7 kg). Where a single authority does not exist,
          figures are rounded mid-range estimates from the sources cited, not false precision.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Water comparisons.</strong> The water charts use water-intensive items instead of
          the carbon ones, all water-consumption figures (the green and blue virtual water used up,
          with grey pollution-dilution water a minor part) from the
          <a href="https://watercalculator.org/footprint/the-hidden-water-in-everyday-products/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Water Footprint Network</a>:
          a slice of bread (~40 L), an apple (~125 L), a cup of coffee (~140 L), an egg (~196 L), a
          glass of milk (~255 L), a cotton T-shirt (~2,700 L), leather shoes (~8,000 L), a pair of
          jeans (~10,000 L), and a beef burger (~1,700 L), plus a
          <a href="https://www.compareandrecycle.co.uk/blog/world-water-week-how-much-water-does-your-smartphone-use" target="_blank" rel="noopener">new smartphone (~12,800 L)</a>.
          The savings are all dietary — eating one fewer burger a week, going vegetarian, or going
          vegan — because almost all of the personal water footprint you can actually cut is the
          consumptive water behind food, not water that runs down a drain and is treated and returned.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </article>
</Base>

